--- body ---
## Context

Follows ticket 0290, which resynced the per-document `*_INCLUDES` sets in `paths.mk` against
each document's real include closure and added
`tests/test_include_graph.py` to keep them honest. The `*_FIGS` sets next to
them have the same defect and are not yet covered.

Measured on 2026-07-27, comparing each set against the figures actually
referenced by the document and everything it includes:

| Set | declared | referenced | declared-not-referenced | referenced-not-declared |
|-----|---------:|-----------:|------------------------:|------------------------:|
| `MANUSCRIPT_FIGS` | 3 | 3 | 0 | 0 |
| `DATAPAPER_FIGS` | 5 | 2 | 3 | 0 |
| `MULTILAYER_FIGS` | 11 | 5 | 7 | 1 |
| `ZOO_SCHEMATICS` + `ZOO_RESULT_FIGS` | 35 | 35 | 0 | 0 |

`MULTILAYER_FIGS` is the one with a genuine hole: `multilayer-detection.qmd`
uses `fig_companion_sensitivity.png`, which no set declares, so editing that
figure does not rebuild the PDF. The rest is over-declaration — a render waits
on Phase-2 work it does not consume.

Three documents have no figure set at all, so their render rules carry no
figure prerequisites: `corpus-report.qmd` (5 figures),
`multilayer-detection-techrep.qmd` (11), and the two slide decks (3 and 2).

0290 already dropped `$(TECHREP_FIGS) $(MULTILAYER_FIGS)` from the technical
report's render rule — the report is the method-zoo document and consumes the
zoo sets — but left `TECHREP_FIGS` itself alone: it still feeds the
`figures-techrep` alias and `ALL_FIGS`, and deciding which of its 12 figures
are still wanted is a content question, not a mechanical one.

## Second finding: figures no document references

Nine Make-built figures are referenced by no deliverable now that the orphaned
includes are gone: `fig_alluvial`, `fig_alluvial_core`, `fig_genealogy`,
`fig_pca_scatter`, `fig_seed_axis_core`, `fig_breakpoints`, `fig_bimodality`,
`fig_kde`, plus the `fig_lexical_tfidf_*` family behind `.lexical_tfidf.stamp`.
They were already unreferenced before 0290 — their only consumers were the
orphaned includes — so this is exposure, not breakage. `make analysis` still
builds them. Ticket 0351 covers one instance of this class (an unwired
interactive corpus figure); this is the general sweep.

Deciding each is a content call: some are worth re-wiring into a document,
some are worth retiring with their producer script and Make rule.

## Relevant files

- `paths.mk` — the `*_FIGS` sets
- `deliverables/*/[a-z]*.mk` — render rules that consume them
- `tests/test_include_graph.py` — the closure walker to extend; it already
  resolves nested includes against the root document's directory
- `Makefile` — `figures-techrep`, `ALL_FIGS`, the `.lexical_tfidf.stamp` rule

## Actions

1. Extend the closure walker to collect figure references (`../_shared/figures/*.png`)
   alongside includes.
2. Resync every `*_FIGS` set to its document's real references; add sets for the
   three documents that have none.
3. Add the guard: each document's figure set equals its references, mirroring
   `test_paths_mk_matches_include_closure`.
4. Put the nine unreferenced figures to the author: re-wire or retire. Retiring
   one means removing its producer script and Make rule too, not just the
   `*_FIGS` entry.

## Test

Red first: `test_paths_mk_matches_figure_references[multilayer-detection.qmd]`
fails on the missing `fig_companion_sensitivity.png` before any fix.

## Invariants

- `make check` stays green; no render rule loses a prerequisite it needs.
- The include-side guard from 0290 keeps passing unchanged.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every `*_FIGS` set matches its document's figure references
- [ ] Every deliverable that references figures has a set
- [ ] A guard fails on drift in either direction
- [ ] The nine unreferenced figures each have a recorded decision
